<InsightArticle title="A small token bill can sit beside a large review queue" description="Frozen AIRH rates price the model calls; explicit fictional assumptions price the review work. A worked guide to keeping cash charges, staff time and uncertain outcomes apart." date="2026-11-14" type="Explainer" readTime="9 min" snapshot="2026-11-14" standfirst="One frozen AIRH price row gives a $35 model-token estimate for 10,000 hypothetical jobs. If a fifth need two minutes of review, that adds about 66.67 hours, valued at $2,000 under an assumed $30 hourly rate.">
<p>The contrast is arithmetic, not a finding about how much AI saves at work. The token rate comes from a saved catalogue record. The job count, review share, review time and hourly value are invented to show how separate parts of a workflow can have different scales. None is a measured staffing requirement or a wage recommendation.</p>
<p>That distinction matters before a team says a workflow is cheap. A provider's charge answers what the service billed. Time spent checking its answers answers another question. The two can be presented together, but adding an assumed money value to staff time does not turn it into a new invoice or prove that staffing costs will fall.</p>
<h2>What the $35 actually buys in this example</h2>
<p>The model-token estimate uses the Mistral Large 4 row from AIRH's frozen 14 November 2026 snapshot, inspected on that date. It records $0.70 per million input tokens and $2.10 per million output tokens. Each job is assumed to send 2,000 input tokens and receive 1,000 output tokens. Multiplying by 10,000 jobs gives $14 of input and $21 of output.</p>
<p>These are historical catalogue rates, not freshly checked offers. Equal fixed lengths are a simplification, and the estimate excludes caching, tools, additional reasoning charges, retries and taxes. It does not establish that the model would complete any of the jobs. The purpose is to give the model-charge column a reproducible starting point before discussing review.</p>
<WorkedTable headers={['Assumed share reviewed','Jobs reviewed','Review hours','Assumed review value: dollars','Model-token dollars']} rows={d.review.map(r=>[`${r.share*100}%`,r.reviewed,Number(r.hours.toFixed(2)),r.reviewCost,r.modelCost])} caption="Invented review scenarios at two minutes per reviewed job and an assumed $30 per hour. Hours are rounded only for display; monetary values use unrounded time. Model rates are frozen AIRH records. These are not observed error rates or labour costs." />
<p>The three rows keep the same model bill and change only the share sent to review. At a twentieth of the queue, 500 jobs take about 16.67 hours under the time assumption. At a fifth, 2,000 take about 66.67 hours. Reviewing the entire queue takes about 333.33 hours. Applying the assumed hourly value gives $500, $2,000 and $10,000 respectively.</p>
<p>The table does not say that the unreviewed answers are correct. Nor does it say that reviewed jobs are failures. Some workflows may review outputs for reasons unrelated to a detected error. Here the review share is simply the fraction routed to a person, with no claim about the reliability of any automatic selection rule.</p>
<h2>Where a review queue gets its definition</h2>
<p>Review needs a concrete activity attached to it. Reading a short answer, comparing it with evidence and correcting an extracted field are different tasks. An estimate that uses one duration for all of them can be useful for teaching, as ours does, but a real plan needs observations of the actual work being costed.</p>
<p>A timing exercise can define its start and finish explicitly. For example, it might begin when a reviewer opens the item and end when the recorded decision is complete. Time spent searching for missing evidence or handing the item to someone else would need an accounting rule. Those choices determine what the measured duration covers.</p>
<p>The outcome categories matter too. A reviewer might accept an answer, repair it, reject it or mark it unresolved. Calling each of those a completed success would hide the amount of work still left. A useful queue record retains the decision and any follow-up, so the cost calculation can be connected to what the user actually received.</p>
<p>Nothing in this example establishes that two minutes is enough for a particular task. A trial would need reviewed cases of the relevant kinds and a stated quality standard. A short review that misses the fault cannot be counted as evidence of an efficient checking process merely because its stopwatch time is low.</p>
<h2>Cash expenditure and assigned time are different accounts</h2>
<p>The assumed $30 hourly value is a way of assigning a money amount to time. It is not a claim about a reviewer’s wage, a contractor quote or a full employment cost. A team can choose another documented basis for its own estimate. The important point is to label what the number represents instead of presenting it as an observed charge.</p>
<p>A reduction in assigned review time does not automatically reduce cash spending. People may use that time on other work while the same payroll remains. The result may still be valuable, but the claim needs to name the resource actually released. A provider invoice and an estimate of reassigned staff time should remain distinguishable in the report.</p>
<p>Conversely, an external review service might create an actual incremental bill. That would need its own price evidence and applicable terms. The teaching hourly assumption here is not a substitute for those. The calculation can show the consequence of a chosen rate without asserting that anyone offers the service at that rate.</p>
<p>Costs from mistakes are another separate category. This article assigns no amount to missed errors or downstream harm. Pricing those would require task-specific evidence and judgement. Adding a convenient number would give the appearance of a complete business case while concealing the very uncertainty the decision depends on.</p>
<h2>Why a lower review share needs outcome evidence</h2>
<p>A proposal to check fewer answers can reduce the table's assumed time. It also changes the checking policy. The relevant comparison needs evidence about the errors left in the accepted queue, rather than only the count routed to a person. Otherwise a weaker gate could look like an improvement simply because it sends less work for inspection.</p>
<p>A gate is the rule that decides what proceeds. It might use a model score, a deterministic check or a human decision; this guide recommends none of those as universally sufficient. A trial needs to explain how the gate works and examine cases on both sides of it. Reviewing only flagged cases leaves the accepted group unexamined.</p>
<p>Comparison with the previous workflow also needs matched scope. If the new process includes verification that the previous process omitted, its higher review time may reflect a different quality standard. If it omits work the previous process required, an apparent saving may reflect that omission. Neither conclusion can be reached from the $35 model bill alone.</p>
<p>The <a href="/transparency/#practitioner-planning">transparency receipt</a> lists the frozen price record, formulas and fictional review assumptions. No review time, acceptance rate or real model result has been measured. The displayed hours remain conditional on the invented queue, and the money values remain conditional on the invented hourly rate.</p>
<p>A useful operating report would keep measured model charges, measured review time and the chosen monetary conversion in separate columns. It could then add a combined figure for a clearly named purpose. Someone reading it later could change the valuation without rewriting the observations, or compare the token bill with the service invoice independently.</p>
<p>Queue size and reviewer capacity can be recorded in hours without converting them into money at all. That may be the clearer planning view when the immediate question is whether the team can clear the work by a deadline. This guide makes no throughput prediction, since its fixed two-minute assumption leaves waiting time, interruptions and differences between cases unmeasured.</p>
<p>An average review duration should also stay attached to its sample. A set of short accepted answers can take a different amount of work from a set of long corrections. If the future queue changes, the old average may no longer describe it. Keeping durations beside outcome categories allows a later estimate to use the relevant mix, while one aggregate hourly figure would conceal the reason for the change. Those measurements would be new evidence; none is supplied by the frozen model-price record.</p>
<p>The example supports a modest conclusion: a small model-token amount does not bound the cost of the whole workflow. Deciding whether the workflow is worthwhile needs evidence about useful completed work and the resources required to obtain it. The rate table supplies one ingredient; the review record supplies another.</p>
</InsightArticle>
